WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.210
Let’s talk about the Taliban.

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:09.300
They’ve been fighting the government and its allies in Afghanistan for 20 years.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.553
Today they seem stronger than ever.

00:00:15.974 --> 00:00:18.268
And now the Americans are going home.

00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:26.109
Biden wants his troops out by September 11. And that could strengthen the Taliban even more.

00:00:27.152 --> 00:00:35.577
So who exactly are the Taliban? How is it that they have so much power? And why are people worried they’ll take over Afghanistan — again?

00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:48.506
To understand the Taliban you need to know what happened in Afghanistan in the 1980s.

00:00:49.841 --> 00:00:58.266
Afghan guerillas called the mujahideen fought a Soviet occupation for nine years. They even got money and weapons from the CIA.

00:00:58.975 --> 00:01:09.736
In 1989 the Soviets pulled out and the next few years were pretty chaotic. By 1992 there was a full-blown civil war with tribal leaders fighting for power.

00:01:10.653 --> 00:01:14.616
Two years later a militia called the Taliban started getting attention.

00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:23.625
Many of its members had studied in conservative religious schools in Afghanistan and across the border in Pakistan and some of them had also fought as mujahideen.

00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:26.086
And they had their own plans for the country.

00:01:48.525 --> 00:01:58.284
By 1996 the Taliban had seized the capital. They declared Afghanistan an Islamic emirate and started imposing their own strict interpretation of Islamic law.

00:02:13.591 --> 00:02:15.885
Then 9/11 happened.

00:02:15.885 --> 00:02:22.100
The US was after al-Qaeda’s leader, Osama bin Laden, who was hiding out in Afghanistan with the Taliban’s help. 

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.270
The Taliban said they wanted proof he was behind the attack.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:29.274
And when they refused to hand him over immediately the Americans invaded.

00:02:33.736 --> 00:02:39.659
Within a couple of months the Taliban were forced out of power and Afghanistan got a new interim government. 

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:44.455
Three years later it got a new constitution and Hamid Karzai was elected president.

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:52.547
While that was going on the Taliban had regrouped. They wanted foreigners out and they wanted back in.

00:02:52.547 --> 00:02:57.468
What followed were years of devastating conflict — and it’s still going on.

00:02:57.468 --> 00:03:00.555
More than 40,000 Afghan civilians killed.

00:03:00.555 --> 00:03:07.478
At least 64,000 Afghan military and police and more than 3,500 international soldiers dead.

00:03:07.478 --> 00:03:13.109
The US alone has spent almost a trillion dollars on the war and reconstruction projects.

00:03:13.109 --> 00:03:20.408
And after all that Afghanistan is still deeply unstable and the Taliban are still a force to reckon with.

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:47.977
Today the Taliban have as many as 85,000 full-time fighters and training camps across the country.

00:03:47.977 --> 00:03:50.855
And the area they control has been growing.

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:56.319
Right now the Taliban control one-fifth of Afghan districts.

00:03:56.319 --> 00:04:04.702
On top of that half of all districts are considered contested which means the Taliban have a strong presence in many of those places too.

00:04:04.702 --> 00:04:07.247
And they control parts of some major highways.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:26.516
They’re also well organised. The Taliban’s leader is Haibatullah Akhundzada.

00:04:26.516 --> 00:04:32.647
He heads a council that oversees about a dozen commissions in charge of things like finance, health and education. 

00:04:32.647 --> 00:04:36.609
Below them are local officials in charge of everyday services.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:40.571
So in a way the Taliban have set up a parallel state. 

00:04:40.571 --> 00:04:44.826
They even run their own courts which can be pretty popular among Afghans.

00:04:59.757 --> 00:05:02.552
All that control has also made them rich.

00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:08.516
According to Taliban members and a UN committee they make close to $1.5 billion a year.

00:05:08.516 --> 00:05:12.603
They’ve always made a lot of money from growing opium poppies and the drug trade.

00:05:12.603 --> 00:05:21.446
But they’ve found even more ways to generate income. Last year they made millions from mining and trading minerals and even producing methamphetamine. 

00:05:21.446 --> 00:05:29.829
They also have their own tax-collection system and receive funding from abroad — although suspected sources, like Pakistan and Iran, deny it.

00:05:31.622 --> 00:05:39.255
Over the years the international community has tried to get the Taliban and the Afghan government talking — or at least to talk about talking.

00:05:39.255 --> 00:05:50.600
There was even a meeting in the Maldives at one point. And during a brief Eid ceasefire in 2018 Taliban and Afghan soldiers were seen mingling in Kabul — even praying together.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:54.270
But overall peace negotiations haven’t achieved much.

00:06:11.621 --> 00:06:17.418
The question now is what will happen when the roughly 10,000 US and NATO soldiers leave?

00:06:17.418 --> 00:06:22.048
Will the Afghan government be able to survive? And what will the Taliban do? 

00:06:27.345 --> 00:06:30.890
Well, the prospects for peace and stability aren't looking great.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:39.190
Even while peace talks have been going on almost 1,800 Afghan civilians have been killed or injured in the first three months of this year. 

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:42.235
That’s almost 30% more than last year.

00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:46.489
There’s also been a wave of assassinations that people blame on the Taliban.

00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:11.264
Afghans are already asking themselves what life might be like if the Taliban take over again.

00:07:11.264 --> 00:07:14.892
Will they rip up the constitution which protects basic human rights.

00:07:25.862 --> 00:07:37.665
In a New York Times op-ed the Taliban tried to clear things up saying they want to “build an Islamic system … where the rights of women that are granted by Islam — from the right to education to the right to work — are protected.”

00:07:37.665 --> 00:07:44.088
And right now there are some places under Taliban control where girls are in school. But not everywhere.

00:07:54.682 --> 00:07:57.268
So how much support is there for the Taliban?

00:07:57.268 --> 00:08:04.442
Well, according to a major public opinion study done in 2019, 85% of people said they had no sympathy for them.

00:08:24.545 --> 00:08:31.010
People may not like them but the Taliban know what they want and they’ve shown that they’re in it for the long haul.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.805
So far their strategy seems to have worked.

00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:48.569
Al Jazeera is always monitoring the situation in Afghanistan and the negotiations here in Doha so check out our website for the latest and YouTube for reports from our correspondents on the ground. 

00:08:48.569 --> 00:08:49.487
I’ll see you next week.

